traditional  partners non-traditional partners to  focus on solutions partnering with the  government's for infrastructure so  public-private partnerships so it's all  about working together to provide these  solutions particularly in congested  urban areas  it's an acquire all of us to work  together and at the same time make sure  that there's a business model and a  benefit for those of us that are that  are that are participating that and a  great solution for customers all right  so the future is bright obviously high  tech and really cool if you talk about  though the now and today your bread and  butter the Ford f-150 I'm driving it  today test testing it out what's your  outlook for this for this you know a lot  of people are saying game changer for  this truck well our new f-150 i'm a bit  biased but it's a technological marvel  and it really we feel is going to change  the industry we're seeing some  reverberations of that right now we're  shipping the vehicles the initial  consumer response is terrific and we're  basically changing the definition of  built ford tough it's it's it's the  strongest most capable vehicle we've  ever put in the marketplace using  technology to solve problems for  customers and that's giving them a more  capable truck and a truck that's gonna  be very fuel efficient so I'm in you  know I'm in my Raptor 2014 rapture I'm  kind of a lead foot i get like 13 miles  a gallon are you gonna get people to  shift up to this more fuel-efficient  truck you're more fuel-efficient  vehicles with gas prices at two dollars  and ninety cents well it's a great  question because when you look across  literally all the segments people still  want great fuel economy you know they  want it they want to make sure their  dollars are stretching further and right  now gas prices are coming down so it's  putting more money in folks pockets but  I think consumers are a lot more smarter  these days they know that gas prices can  go down they could go up and they want  to make sure the vehicles that they're  buying that they're getting the very  best fuel economy and that's why we're  taking the approach with the f-150 and  all of our vehicle lines so they know  that Ford fuel economy is a reason to  buy you know a big picture if you step  back and look at cheaper gas prices have  to get people i would think out to buy  new cars because it's less expensive to  run them how does it though affect your  whole industry to see oil coming down  this sharply this quickly well i think  to your point it is a it's  it's a bonus for all the consumers out  there it immediately it's almost like an  immediate tax cut it puts more money in  their pockets you know clearly we think  that will stimulate the economy will  have a benefit in the auto industry so  we're looking at that as an opportunity  but also at the same time not taking our  eye off the fact that we shouldn't get  diluted by oh well fuel economy is not  important to consumers going forward it  will be because when we provide that  solution they'll get better fuel economy  and they'll get more money in their  pockets so they can do what they want  whether it's send their kids to college  or buy more things for themselves will  it help the situation in Europe I mean  things look great here in the US over in  Europe it's a different story even if  the industry kind of bounces back Ford  still projecting losses for next year  well when you look at Europe clearly  with as we're seeing here in the US  depending upon the study you see the  drop in gas prices could add two three  even four tenths of percentage points of  gdp we think it will be helpful in  Europe because again it is a it goes  right to the bottom line of folks but  clearly the market in Europe is still  it's still challenge we do see growth  this year minimally our projection for  next year is a bit more growth but we  have to watch it very closely because  it's been plateauing of it what can you  do differently I mean you're sending  Farley over there I know Jim parlays  gonna go and run your division there  what can for do differently to try and  beat out competitors with this with such  a minimal bounce back well our whole  approach there is implementing what we  call our transformation plan out there  and it's around building our brand it's  around introducing a full family of  products and we're doing that and  particularly see you these and small s  you'd small and medium-sized SUVs  because that segment is growing wildly  there and we it's a sweet spot for us as  a company and then finally working on  costs so we're gonna continue to work  our plan their continue to look at the  business environment and make sure we're  doing the right things to get the  business back to profitability how do  you feel about the situation in Russia I  mean you've you've been all over the  globe just to remind people you know  you've run things in Europe you've been  in Asia what do you think when something  like this happens  the global turmoil and I just noticed  you you're opening a third plant with a  joint venture in Russia I mean it seems  like a strange difficult time to expand  their well even in the most difficult  times and Russia is a very difficult  market right now still we have to step  back and when you look at even the the  diminished expectations of the  marketplace it's around to 2.2 million  units that still makes it one of the  biggest markets in Europe and there's  never a bad time to introduce great  product into a marketplace so the plant  that we're just opening is introducing  our EcoSport which is a small SUV  perfectly suited for the Russian market  place and we want to serve our Russian  customers so as we look at that business  there we want to make sure we're dealing  with the business environment continuing  to work our plan work our way through it  play through it because we think it's  it's an important market and will be  important market going forward I want to  finally ask you about the cicadas  situation here we talked about it last  time I was in Detroit will you continue  to work with Takata will you recall all  of the vehicles that are equipped with  the faulty airbags what do you think  about the supplier well we've been we've  been working with nitsa on and taking  the appropriate actions because you know  for us very simply the safety of our  customers is of the utmost importance so  we're working with its on that and you  know we're continuing to work with with  Takata in terms of some of the  technologies as well as working with  some of the other suppliers for those  type of systems in the in the automotive  industry and we'll continue to do that  but will you recall that all the cars  that are equipped with those Takata  airbags well we've recalled all the ones  in the affected areas that nitsa has  asked us to do so and we'll continue to  look at it going forward as we look at  customer experience on that but safety  is very important and will continue to  be proactive on this as we go forward  alright mark thanks so much for spending  time with us appreciated mark fields k  CEO of the ford motor company here in  Detroit
 

